Q: How to simplify checking whether a reference variable is null? A: <p>I ended up using <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79892295/how-to-simplify-checking-whether-a-reference-variable-is-null/79892482#79892482">Dai's idea</a> of an extension property on all <code>struct</code> types. It has the disadvantage, mentioned by Matthew Watson <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79892295/how-to-simplify-checking-whether-a-reference-variable-is-null#comment140988529_79892295">in a comment</a>, of appearing on every struct regardless of whether it's actually a reference, but this disadvantage is vastly outweighed by the readability benefit of the extension IMHO. I changed the name of the extension to my liking, and added another one to cover both cases <code>is null</code>/<code>is not null</code>. I also decorated it with the <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.runtime.compilerservices.methodimploptions" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>AggressiveInlining</code></a> attribute, so that (hopefully) the extension doesn't affect the performance of my code. Finally I put the two extensions in a dedicated namespace, so that I can enable them easily on the few code files that need it, without affecting the rest of my code base.</p>
<pre><code>namespace NullRef;

public static class NullableReferenceExtensions
{
    extension&lt;T&gt;(ref T self) where T : struct
    {
        public bool IsNull
        {
            [MethodImpl(MethodImplOptions.AggressiveInlining)]
            get =&gt; Unsafe.IsNullRef(ref self);
        }

        public bool IsNotNull
        {
            [MethodImpl(MethodImplOptions.AggressiveInlining)]
            get =&gt; !Unsafe.IsNullRef(ref self);
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Usage example:</p>
<pre><code>using NullRef;

ref int result = ref GetRef();
if (result.IsNotNull)
{
    // ...
}
</code></pre>
